WEBVTT

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.100
Settle-in, this is unintended consequences, episode 2.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.580
Monday, a fresh case to chew on as the week begins.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.260
In the summer of 1935, staff at the Meringue Sugar Experiment Station near Gordonvale

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.880
in Northern Queensland opened crates containing 102 live cane totes shipped from Hawaii.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.020
The animals were placed in the surrounding sugar cane fields with the expectation that

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.380
they would hunt at night and clear out the beetles chewing through the crop roots.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.700
Within a few years, the totes had moved well beyond those fields.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.840
By the time their numbers reached into the millions, their original agricultural problem

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.620
had been replaced by something far larger and harder to contain.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:53.420
Queensland's sugar cane industry in the early 1930s faced pressure from several beetle

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:53.920
species.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.860
The grayback cane beetle stood out as particularly destructive.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:07.640
Its larvae attacked the roots while the adults fed on the leaves, yields dropped across thousands of hectares as a result.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.560
Chemical controls remained rudimentary and expensive at that time.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.920
Manual collection of the beetles could not keep pace with the damage.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.400
Researchers looked to Hawaii for answers.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.980
Cain totes had been introduced there in 1932.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:32.640
They seemed to provide a low-cost, self-replicating way to handle similar pests in sugar and pineapple fields.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:39.240
Reginald Montgomery, an entomologist with the Bureau of Sugar Experiment stations,

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.540
advocated for bringing the totes to Australia.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:48.240
He believed they would forge on the ground at night when the beetles were most vulnerable.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.400
Ecology as a science was still developing in that era.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.200
Attention stayed on immediate crop protection needs.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.120
Importing a predator from another tropical region appeared rational and forward-thinking.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.160
Farmers and station managers backed the plan.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.660
It offered a way to cut down on labor-intensive methods.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.640
The approach also matched the pattern of biological control efforts used elsewhere in the world.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.900
The first 102 totes arrived in June 1935.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.500
They were held at the Meringa station for observation and limited breeding.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.900
Releases into nearby fields came next.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.740
Early reports noted that the totes adapted quickly to the local climate.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.360
They showed clear signs of establishing populations.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.300
More shipments arrived in the late 1930s.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.940
Releases expanded to districts around Cairns and further south.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.180
Proponents saw the survival and reproduction as proof the method worked.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.520
The Bureau maintained support into the early 1940s.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:02.720
Some scientists and naturalists outside the industry raised concerns about effects on native wildlife.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.400
Those concerns drew little notice while the focus remained on the crop.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.020
By the end of the decade, totes appeared beyond the original sites.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.100
This spread was viewed mainly as successful establishment.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.420
The totes did not turn out to be effective at controlling the cane beetles.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.560
Adult beetles often fed higher on the plants or flew to new fields.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.980
The totes stayed on the ground and chose easier prey instead.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.080
This mismatch and behavior meant the beetles often escaped predation.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.540
The totes focused on insects and amphibians that were more accessible.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.640
They consumed a wide range of native insects and small frogs.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.860
Other ground-dwelling creatures also became part of their diet.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:54.040
Local food webs changed in ways that took time to notice.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.420
The breeding rate exceeded all expectations.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:03.240
Females could produce clutches of up to 30,000 eggs in the wet season.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.640
Populations grew exponentially after leaving the plantations.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:14.220
The Bufoxin toxin from glands on their backs and necks caused the greatest harm.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.820
Many Australian predators had never encountered this toxin before.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.160
Northern quills, goanas, and various snakes tried to eat the totes.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:30.740
They experienced rapid heart failure as a result. Predators had no evolutionary history with this

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.940
particular toxin. Sharp declines followed in these predator populations.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.720
The absence of these animals changed the balance in the local environment.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:46.620
In some areas, quall numbers dropped by more than 80% within a few years.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.380
The loss of these predators allowed insect and small mammal populations to fluctuate in unexpected

00:04:53.380 --> 00:05:00.040
ways. Some insect groups increased while others decreased depending on the new dynamics.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.040
Toads competed with native frogs for breeding sites and food resources.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.740
This added pressure to amphibian communities dealing with other habitat issues.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:17.760
Rural residents started seeing large numbers of toads near homes and water tanks.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.280
Livestock areas also saw frequent encounters.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.420
Informal culling efforts began but made little difference to the growing numbers.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.400
The scale of the tot population made such efforts ineffective over large areas.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:37.380
By the 1970s, the totes had reached the northern territory.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:44.280
Westward expansions sometimes covered tens of kilometers in a single year during wet seasons.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.120
Favorable conditions during the rains supported this fast movement.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.560
Predator and prey relationships shifted across wide regions as a result.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.480
Certain crows and kites developed a behavior of flipping totes to avoid the toxin.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.580
They ate only the safe parts of the animals.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:08.260
This adaptation helped some birds survive in the changed environment.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:13.180
By most accounts, the population now exceeds 200 million individuals.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:18.600
The range covers more than a million square kilometers in northern Australia.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.520
By the 1950s and 1960s, the full extent of the spread became evident.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:31.280
Early efforts like collection drives and trapping failed to slow the totes of bads.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:38.260
The Commonwealth's scientific and industrial research organization began exploring new methods.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.740
Pharamone-based traps were one option under consideration.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.840
Viruses and genetic techniques aimed at limiting breeding also received attention.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.480
Some methods showed promise in limited trials.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:59.400
Scaling these solutions to the large occupied areas raised technical and regulatory challenges.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.800
The invasion front keeps moving westward.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.820
Toads have now been recorded in parts of the Kimberly region.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.620
They approach the edge of more arid zones in western Australia.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.980
A few native species show signs of adaptation over time.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.020
Some snake populations have developed longer jaws.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:25.280
This allows them to eat smaller totes and reduce talks and exposure.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.820
Management today aims to slow further spread.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.360
Community monitoring programs play a key role in this effort.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.200
Targeted removal happens in areas of high conservation value.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.240
Full eradication is not the current goal.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.920
The totes have become a permanent part of the northern Australian landscape.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.680
Research continues on their ecological role and options for long-term containment.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:57.140
Efforts to manage biological systems often reveal the need to assess whether

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:00.620
an introduced organism will stick to its intended role.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:08.560
Many such organisms proved to be generalists capable of thriving under a variety of conditions.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:14.920
They may also reproduce at rates that allow rapid expansion beyond the original area.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:22.320
Thuro testing for these generalist traits and high reproductive capacity before any release

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.000
can reduce the chance of persistent problems.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:31.760
Living systems also feature numerous indirect connections between different species and resources.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:37.520
Introducing or removing one element can therefore alter predator prey balances

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:43.440
in unexpected directions. Competition for food and breeding sites may shift as well.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.800
These adjustments frequently take many years or decades to become fully apparent.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.320
Anyone planning interventions in agriculture, conservation, or technology

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.300
benefits from mapping out possible escape routes and long-term adaptability in advance.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.900
This kind of careful preparation requires time and resources at the start.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:11.980
Yet it can prevent far more extensive issues from developing later on.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:18.660
Consider current proposals for gene drives or new biological controls in other regions.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:25.060
These approaches aim to address pests or disease vectors in agriculture and public health.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:31.520
What tests for behavioral flexibility and reproductive success outside the target scope

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.360
would you want to see completed before any move from trials to widespread release?

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:42.720
That wraps today's case. The lessons travel further when you share them.

